WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.829 --> 00:00:03.209
The flat roof of a parts manufacturer.

2
00:00:03.924 --> 00:00:05.840
Two jobs are planned here today.

3
00:00:06.886 --> 00:00:11.135
Non-routine, high-risk work needs a permit to work: the hazards known,

4
00:00:11.379 --> 00:00:14.143
the area ready, and clear who is in control.

5
00:00:14.863 --> 00:00:18.033
Open permits hang on a board in the control room.

6
00:00:19.025 --> 00:00:23.774
The first job is hot work: anything that makes sparks or flame, such as welding,

7
00:00:23.913 --> 00:00:25.004
grinding or cutting.

8
00:00:25.741 --> 00:00:28.945
Welders from a contractor weld a bracket to the platform.

9
00:00:29.947 --> 00:00:34.812
A confined space, like this tank with its narrow way in, needs a permit too.

10
00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:40.947
It can hold too little oxygen, or toxic or flammable gas, and you cannot see that.

11
00:00:41.706 --> 00:00:43.204
So first a gas test.

12
00:00:44.554 --> 00:00:49.152
The permit lists the job and the place, the hazards, and the isolations,

13
00:00:49.361 --> 00:00:52.542
with a reference to the lockout on every isolation point.

14
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:58.574
Then the tests, the PPE, how long it is valid, today from eight to four,

15
00:00:58.748 --> 00:00:59.770
and the signatures.

16
00:01:00.788 --> 00:01:03.586
The issuer from production controls this area.

17
00:01:04.296 --> 00:01:08.731
He walks it with the welding crew leader; signing without looking often goes wrong.

18
00:01:09.716 --> 00:01:14.349
Flammable material is removed or covered, within the distance in the site rule.

19
00:01:15.058 --> 00:01:20.050
A fire blanket goes over the power unit, and an extinguisher within reach.

20
00:01:21.119 --> 00:01:23.836
Then the issuer signs: the area is ready.

21
00:01:24.529 --> 00:01:28.871
The crew leader is the holder: he accepts the permit and signs too.

22
00:01:30.146 --> 00:01:34.465
The welder welds the bracket under the platform; the sparks fall onto the roof.

23
00:01:35.515 --> 00:01:39.613
Next to him stands the fire watch: someone who only watches for fire,

24
00:01:39.776 --> 00:01:41.448
with the extinguisher at hand.

25
00:01:42.486 --> 00:01:45.911
The holder keeps the permit at the job and briefs his crew.

26
00:01:47.238 --> 00:01:51.847
Then the second job: two technicians come to replace the motor of the roof fan.

27
00:01:52.868 --> 00:01:55.329
Their work order says which job to do where,

28
00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.768
with which measures: up the fixed ladder.

29
00:01:58.494 --> 00:02:00.305
It says nothing about a welder.

30
00:02:01.337 --> 00:02:06.504
At the ladder they do their LMRA, the last-minute risk analysis: a short check,

31
00:02:06.677 --> 00:02:09.638
just before starting, by the people doing the work.

32
00:02:10.403 --> 00:02:12.597
One to three minutes, out loud.

33
00:02:13.653 --> 00:02:15.267
Do I know what to do, and how?

34
00:02:15.973 --> 00:02:17.703
What can go wrong here and now?

35
00:02:18.401 --> 00:02:19.667
Are the measures in place?

36
00:02:20.363 --> 00:02:22.581
Do I have the right tools and PPE?

37
00:02:23.285 --> 00:02:24.225
Are others affected?

38
00:02:25.251 --> 00:02:27.422
The LMRA comes from VCA,

39
00:02:27.631 --> 00:02:30.928
the Dutch safety checklist and certificate for contractors:

40
00:02:31.079 --> 00:02:33.145
firms hired to work on your site.

41
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:37.484
It has rained, so the roof is wet and slippery.

42
00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:41.036
And they hear a contractor welding on the same roof,

43
00:02:41.176 --> 00:02:43.022
which was not on their work order.

44
00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:47.256
The LMRA catches what has changed since the planning.

45
00:02:47.974 --> 00:02:51.062
If an answer is no or unclear, you do not start.

46
00:02:52.169 --> 00:02:56.604
They call the contractor’s supervisor, and the issuer of the welding permit.

47
00:02:57.688 --> 00:03:00.451
They agree that the welder stays at the other end,

48
00:03:00.614 --> 00:03:03.667
and check the guardrail on the roof edge by the fan.

49
00:03:04.372 --> 00:03:05.916
An hour later they start.

50
00:03:06.988 --> 00:03:09.449
So jobs in one area are coordinated:

51
00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:13.304
two permits that each make sense can together create a hazard.

52
00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:16.867
Is there, in your reasonable judgement,

53
00:03:16.936 --> 00:03:20.965
serious danger so immediate that you cannot wait for the inspectorate?

54
00:03:21.774 --> 00:03:24.491
Then you may stop the work: the stop-work right.

55
00:03:25.539 --> 00:03:28.302
It is in article 29 of the Arbowet.

56
00:03:29.082 --> 00:03:32.066
You keep your pay, tell your supervisor right away,

57
00:03:32.298 --> 00:03:36.826
and the stop is reported to the Netherlands Labour Authority as soon as possible.

58
00:03:37.823 --> 00:03:42.177
Many sites give everyone a stop rule, visitors and contractors too.

59
00:03:42.894 --> 00:03:45.971
Stopping is not failing; carrying on when unsure is.

60
00:03:46.695 --> 00:03:48.216
Whoever stops is thanked.

61
00:03:49.275 --> 00:03:53.211
A permit does not replace the LMRA: the permit sets the plan,

62
00:03:53.409 --> 00:03:55.313
the LMRA checks it on the spot.

63
00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.768
A card signed in two seconds checks nothing: name the risks together, out loud.

64
00:04:01.541 --> 00:04:05.245
Crews measured or paid per job skip the LMRA first.

65
00:04:05.994 --> 00:04:08.954
The test: is work ever stopped after an LMRA?

66
00:04:10.296 --> 00:04:12.467
At half past two the welding is done.

67
00:04:13.138 --> 00:04:15.332
The fire watch stays for the set time,

68
00:04:15.507 --> 00:04:19.988
because smouldering material can flare up later; the site rule says how long.

69
00:04:20.966 --> 00:04:25.668
The holder hands the permit back in person, and the issuer walks the area again,

70
00:04:25.888 --> 00:04:27.200
under the platform too.

71
00:04:28.199 --> 00:04:30.672
Only then does the issuer close the permit,

72
00:04:30.893 --> 00:04:34.991
and after that the isolations come off and the power unit runs again.

73
00:04:35.984 --> 00:04:41.661
If a job runs into the next shift, the permit is extended or handed over in person.

74
00:04:42.707 --> 00:04:47.839
On Piper Alpha, an oil platform, this went wrong in 1988.

75
00:04:48.618 --> 00:04:51.393
A pump under maintenance, without its safety valve,

76
00:04:51.486 --> 00:04:55.631
was started, because the permit had not been passed on at shift change.

77
00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.786
167 people died.
